{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_50","type":"reasoning","text":"Now, thinking about chunk 5: main() orchestration + output.\n\nmain():\n1. Load reference, encode, build index.\n2. occ_ref: scatter back to reference order: occ_ref = np.empty(n_km, uint16); occ_ref[pos] = occ.\n3. Load R1, R2.\n4. Map both ends → starts, strands, matches, clusters, rcc, aux.\n5. Depth analysis + call interval.\n6. Pair analysis + clustering of discordant pairs.\n7. Split reads: define a search region around the depth interval: [depth_left_edge - 200kb, depth_right_edge + 200kb]. Primary split search on both ends, then partial-split search.\n8. Integrate breakpoints; rounding; outputs.\n\nAlso QC metrics:\n- Number of reads, number of mapped reads, mapping rate, average match identity of mapped reads.\n- Average depth = total mapped bases / ref_len.\n- Insert size statistics.\n- Depth ratio inside the interval.\n- Evidence summary.\n\nAlso: for qc.json, record the full list of discordant pairs (small number) and split reads (small number).\n\nRegion for split search: needs to include both flanks: region = (max(0, left_edge-300_000), min(ref_len, right_edge+300_000)). In find_split_reads, filter with `region[0] <= d1 and d2 <= region[1]` — d1 is near the left flank, d2 is near the right flank. ✓\n\nIntegration logic:\n```python\nsplits = primary + secondary\n# Keep those near the depth interval and consistent in size (within 2x of each other?)\nif splits:\n    left_raw = int(np.median([x['J_left'] for x in splits]))\n    right_raw = int(np.median([x['J_right'] for x in splits]))\n    bp_method = 'split_reads'\nelif disc clusters:\n    best = max(clusters, key=lambda c: c['n'])\n    left_raw = best['left_lower_bound']; right_raw = best['right_upper_bound']\n    bp_method='discordant_pairs(bracket)'\nelse:\n    left_raw = interval['depth_left_edge']; right_raw = interval['depth_right_edge']\n    bp_method='read_depth'\n```\n\nRounding function: def round100(x): return int(np.floor(x/100_000 + 0.5)) * 100_000.\n\n1-based coordinates: pos_1 = left_raw (the last retained base), end_1 = right_raw (the last deleted base). Rounding: start_100kb = round100(pos_1), end_100kb = round100(end_1). size_bp = end_100kb - start_100kb.\n\nGuard: if end_100kb <= start_100kb (very small), keep at least 100kb? The deletion is said to be large; not a problem.\n\nContents of qc.json:\n\n```python\nqc = {\n \"task\": \"locate large deletion in shallow PE hg38 data\",\n \"coordinate_convention\": \"1-based; start_100kb = last retained base before deletion, end_100kb = last deleted base; size_bp = end_100kb - start_100kb\",\n \"inputs\": {...},\n \"reference\": {...},\n \"alignment\": {...},\n \"insert_size\": {...},\n \"coverage\": {...},\n \"depth_segmentation\": {...},\n \"breakpoint_evidence\": {\n    \"raw_estimates\": {...},\n    \"rounded\": {...},\n    \"precision\": {...}\n },\n \"deletion\": {...}\n}\n```\n\nreport.md: structured text.\n\nAlso, deletion.tsv: columns are chrom, start_100kb, end_100kb, size_bp, supporting_signals.\n\nsupporting_signals example: \"read_depth(12 bins, ratio=0.02, homozygous);discordant_pairs=2;split_reads=3\".\n\nWrite chunk 5."}}
