{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_56","type":"reasoning","text":"There's one bug: inside main() I'm calling `split_from_partials(codes1, (q1, m1, cr1, cs1, cp1), ...)`, but the signature of split_from_partials is `(codes, rcc, aux, ref_codes, index, label, region)`. Need to check the signature: I defined it as `def split_from_partials(codes, rcc, aux, ref_codes, index, label, region)`. In main I'm calling it with only codes and aux (no rcc). Let's fix it: either change the signature to not require rcc (since q_all already contains the aligned orientation, rcc isn't used), or fix the call site. Looking at split_from_partials's body: it uses q_all (aligned sequence), so rcc is unused. Fix the signature: `def split_from_partials(codes, aux, ref_codes, index, label, region)` — remove rcc. Edit in Python.\n\nAlso, check that find_split_reads's signature matches the call: `find_split_reads(codes1, rcc1, clusters1, ref_codes, index, \"R1\", region)` ✓ signature `(codes, rcc, clusters, ref_codes, index, label, region=None)` ✓.\n\nAlso, `split_from_partials` has some weird leftover lines:\n```python\n            if not match[s:s + 10].any() or m_all[j] < s:\n                # suffix is not already explained by position A\n                pass\n```\nThis does nothing (a no-op). Intent: skip split points where the suffix is already well-matched at A (i.e., the read is fully aligned and s is simply an internal point). If match[s:] is mostly True, then this s isn't a real split point. Better approach: require that the match fraction of match[s:] in position A is below 0.5 (i.e., the suffix isn't explained by A). Replace that block with:\n```python\n            if match[s:].sum() > 0.5 * (READ_LEN - s):\n                continue  # suffix already explained by alignment at A\n```\nAlso, the loop `for s in range(40, READ_LEN-40)` requires prefix match ≥ 0.85*s at A — for the true split point s*, prefix match is high; for s < s*, prefix match is also high. So the loop finds the smallest s satisfying the prefix condition and breaks on the first success where the suffix maps elsewhere. If the suffix maps elsewhere, that's fine. But false positives are possible at small s: if s=40 < s*, prefix read[:40] matches at A (no problem), suffix read[40:] = remaining prefix part + true suffix: does read[40:s*] match at B? It matches at A, not at B. The lookup seeds of read[40:] would mostly support position A+(40) on the diagonal, not elsewhere. _lookup_segments returns clusters with ≥2 votes at the reference start of that segment — the suffix-internal diagonal d = refpos - o'; the segment's seeds: those within read[40:s*] vote for d = A - 40 (in the global diagonal sense)… hmm wait, _lookup_segments returns diagonal d such that refpos = d + g (where g = segment start within the read = s). Seeds within the segment at internal offset o': refpos = A + (s + o' - s)?? Let me recompute: seed at global offset o (o ∈ [s, s*−K]) matches ref at position A + o. Internal offset o' = o − s. Internal diagonal d = refpos − o' = A + o − (o − s) = A + s. And for the true suffix part (o ≥ s*): refpos = B + (o − s*), d = B + o − s* − (o − s) = B − s* + s. So there are two diagonal groups within the segment: d1' = A+s (votes from the still-aligned part) and d2' = B − s* + s (votes from the true suffix). _lookup_segments returns the cluster sorted by vote count; if the still-aligned part has more votes, cl[0] = A+s → B' = d + s = A + 2s… then check B' <= A + s + 1000 → A+2s vs A+s+1000: s=40 → A+80 < A+1040 → rejected ✓. If the true suffix has more votes (s* − 40 < suffix length), cl[0] = B − s* + s → B' = B − s* + 2s. If s=40 and s*=70: B' = B − 30 → verification against ref[B−30 : ...] with suf = read[40:] → first 30 bp don't match (that's ref[A+40:A+70] vs ref[B−30:B]), match rate = (L−s−30)/(L−s)… 80/110 = 0.73 < 0.85 → rejected ✓. Good — false positives are filtered out by verification; the loop continues to larger s. But wait, `break` happens only after success; rejection lets the loop continue. Good. But there's a subtle point: at s slightly smaller than s*, match[s:].sum() > 0.5*(L−s) → skipped by my new guard ✓ efficient.\n\nAlso, the condition for the primary route in find_split_reads `if region is not None and not (region[0] <= d1 and d2 <= region[1])` — d1 and d2 are 0-based starts on the reference; region brackets the deletion ±300kb ✓.\n\nOne more check — the s range in find_split_reads: s_lo = max(max(offs1)+K, 30), s_hi = min(min(offs2)+K−1, READ_LEN−30). Hmm, derivation: a seed at offset o votes for cluster 1 ⟺ fully within prefix ⟺ o+K ≤ s → s ≥ o+K → s ≥ max_off1 + K ✓. A seed votes for cluster 2 ⟺ o ≥ s → s ≤ min_off2. I wrote s_hi = min(offs2) + K − 1 — wrong; it should be s ≤ min_off2 → s_hi = min(min_off2, READ_LEN−30). Fix. Actually hmm, why did I write +K−1? Mistake. Fix: s_hi = min(min(offs2), READ_LEN − 30).\n\nWait, but there's an edge case: seeds at offset o where o < s < o+K (spanning the junction) don't vote for either cluster; if all seeds on one side span the junction, that cluster may have 0 votes and fall below MIN_CLUSTER. Example: s=75: seeds 0..60 → prefix seeds 0,10,...,50 (5 seeds with o+K≤75 → o ≤ 55: 0,10,20,30,40,50 = 6 seeds); suffix seeds o≥75: 80,90,...,130 = 6 seeds. No problem.\n\nAlso, the condition in find_split_reads's inner loop `if d1 + s > ref_len or d2 + READ_LEN > ref_len: continue` — the suffix window ref[d2+s : d2+READ_LEN] needs d2 + READ_LEN ≤ ref_len ✓; the prefix needs d1+s ≤ ref_len ✓.\n\nAdditionally, in find_split_reads I'm iterating over all cluster pairs per read including clusters from repetitive noise; the region filter and verification handle it.\n\nEfficiency of find_split_reads: for each read with ≥2 clusters, I recompute _seed_diag for 14 offsets × pairs. Number of reads with ≥2 clusters may be in the hundreds (repetitive reads). Fine.\n\nOne more potential bug: in map_end, `clusters = (u_rid[counts >= MIN_CLUSTER], ...)`. u_rid etc. were computed before