{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_84","type":"reasoning","text":"This is extremely suggestive. Looking at the fine-scale profile:\n\n- Bins 2000–2133 (20.0M–21.34M): basically all near zero! dep 0–35, ratios 0–0.36.\n- Bin 2135 (21.35–21.36M): dep=85, ratio 0.424 — transition.\n- Bins 2136 onward (21.36M–): normal to high (ratios 0.9–2.7).\n\nWait, so this deletion extends from 20.0M all the way to ~21.35M?! But earlier coarse bins 210 (21.0–21.1M, dep=1835, ratio 1.134) and 211 (21.1–21.2M, dep=1766, ratio 0.988) looked normal! Now in the fine-scale bins I see 2110–2119 all near zero… Contradiction?\n\nHmm wait, let me look at the truncated middle section (2090–2115 is cut off: \"2090: pos=209…1543 chars truncated…0 dep=27 ratio=0.155 mfrac=0.71\"). The truncated part covers ~2090–2115. Then \"2116: dep=5 ratio=0.033\". Hmm, and the line just before 2116 is truncated: \"...0 dep=27 ratio=0.155 mfrac=0.71\" — this is probably bin 2115.\n\nBut coarse bin 210 has dep=1835 and 211 has dep=1766. Sum of fine bins 2100–2109 should equal 1835, and 2110–2119 should equal 1766. Fine bins 2116–2119 have dep 5+1+28+3=37. So fine bins 2110–2115 must sum to ~1729! And fine bins 2100–2109 sum to 1835. So there IS a normal-depth block at 21.0–21.16M, then it drops again at ~21.16–21.34M, then recovers at 21.35M.\n\nSo the actual structure is:\n- Deletion 1: [20.0M, 21.0M) — ratio ~0.\n- Normal: [21.0M, ~21.16M).\n- Deletion 2 (or continuation?): [~21.16M, ~21.35M) — ratio ~0–0.15.\n- Then recovery from 21.35M, with a partial dip at 21.35–21.36M (0.424), then bins 2146–2155 are zero again?! (2146–2155: dep 0,0,2,0,0,1,3,0,26 — ratios ~0–0.27), then normal from 2156.\n\nWhoa. So multiple depleted blocks: [20.0,21.0), [21.16,21.35), [21.46,21.55)? And bins 2137–2145 are elevated (1.5–5.0x)?! This looks like a complex rearrangement region… or these are mappability-driven artifacts: bins with low mfrac (0.15–0.5) have unstable ratios. Bin 2139: ratio 5.059, mfrac=0.15 — few mappable positions; a small cluster of reads inflates the ratio.\n\nHmm, let me think about what could produce: 1Mb of zero + normal patch + zero patch + high-ratio patch + zero patch…\n\nAlternative: my mappability normalization could be wrong in duplicated regions. If a region is present in two copies in the reference, unique-seed reads from that family cannot be placed (excluded by occ≤16) but reads containing unique sequence adjacent to the duplication will map — dep reflects true single-copy mappability. My normalization uses expected = mb × global_rate, where mb counts read starts with all k-mers having occ≤16. In a duplicated region mb is low → expected is low → ratio can be inflated (if reads nonetheless map there thanks to anchors of nearby unique k-mers) or deflated…\n\nHmm wait, actually there's a subtlety: my seed filter drops seeds with occ>16, but a read needs only ≥1 cluster of ≥… well, the best cluster regardless of vote count; verification requires 130/150 matches. Reads from a two-copy repeat: seeds vote on two diagonals, best cluster might be ~half the seeds → still placed at one of the copies → dep gets contributions from reads originating from both copies → dep up to 2× the expected (which assumes only the local copy) → ratio ~2. This would explain ratios of 1.5–2.7 in bins 2137–2145, 2156–2190: those are duplicated regions where reads from two copies pile up.\n\nAnd the near-zero blocks: [20.0,21.0), [21.16,21.35), [21.46,21.55): truly deleted in the sample? Three deletions? Hmm, \"a large deletion\" — singular.\n\nWait wait wait. Let me reconsider. What if the sample genome has a complex structure: a large deletion with an insertion of sequence from elsewhere? Or… what if the true deletion is [20,000,000, 21,000,000) AND the sample additionally has sequence that in the reference lies at 21.0–21.16M but… hmm.\n\nLet me think about the split-read patterns again:\n- Pattern B: J_left ~21.1289–21.1290M, J_right ~21.1303–21.1305M, span 1466. This is inside the \"normal\" 21.0–21.16M patch. Hmm.\n- Pattern A: J_left ~20.343–20.354M (inside deletion 1), J_right ~21.172–21.183M (inside the 21.16–21.35M depleted block!).\n\nInteresting!! Pattern A's J_right falls in the second depleted block [21.16, 21.35M), and J_left falls inside deletion 1. And spans are nearly constant 828,941.\n\nNew hypothesis: the reference has a segmental duplication: block X is present in two copies:\n- Copy 1: ~[20,343,000, 20,343,000+828,941) = [20.343M, 21.172M) — inside deletion 1's region and beyond,\n- Copy 2: ~[21,172,000, 21,172,000+828,941) = [21.172M, 22.001M).\n\nHmm interesting: 20,343,118 + 828,949 = 21,172,067. Pattern A's read R2#263609: J_left=20343118, J_right=21172067 — exactly 828,949 apart. And R1#62835: 20343284 → 21172233 (also +828,949). R1#172894: 20352150→21181091 (+828,941). R2#62465: 20352154→21181095 (+828,941). R2#175537: 20350577→21179522 (+828,945). R2#221781: 20352776→21181716 (+828,940). R2#276948: 20354106→21183037 (+828,931).\n\nHmm, the offset isn't perfectly constant: 828,949; 828,949; 828,941; 828,941; 828,945; 828,940; 828,931. Varies over 18bp. And the J_left values cluster into two groups: ~20,343,1xx–20,343,2xx (two reads) and ~20,350,5xx–20,354,1xx (five reads).\n\nIf the reference has two copies of an ~829kb duplicated block with ~99.999% identity, a read from copy 1's locus could have its prefix map to copy 1 and suffix map to copy 2 (if the read spans a position where the copies diverge…). Hmm no — a single 150bp read cannot contain enough divergence to anchor both segments to different copies; if the copies are identical, the segments would map to both copies equally (ambiguity), and my algorithm picks clusters arbitrarily. If the copies have divergence, a read originating from copy 1 would map fully to copy 1.\n\nOK, here's a cleaner thought experiment. Instead, suppose the SAMPLE genome has a deletion of one copy of a tandem duplication…\n\nNo wait. Let me step back and think about the sample structure that would produce all observations:\n\nObservations:\n1. Depth zero: [20.0M, 21.0M) (fine bins 2000–2099, including 2089–2099 with mfrac 0.23–0.67 — all zero).\n2. Depth normal: [21.0M, ~21.16M) (fine bins 2100–2115, sum ~3600 reads over 160kb ≈ 22.5 reads/10kb — matches genome-wide ~19–20/10kb × mfrac… reasonable).\n3. Depth zero: [~21.16M, ~21.35M).\n4. Depth zero: [~21.46M, ~21.55M).\n5. Elevated depth: [21.36M, 21.46M), ratios 0.9–5.0 (mfrac 0.15–0.89).\n6. Split reads exactly at 20,000,000|21,000,000 (pattern C).\n7. Split reads at ~21.129M|21.130M span 1466 (pattern B) — inside the normal patch 21.0–21.16M.\n8. Split reads pattern A: left ~20.343–20.354M, right ~21.172–21.183M.\n\nHmm, observations 3, 4, 5 suggest the region 21.1–21.6M is complex in the reference (duplications/gaps?), and my mapper/normalization is struggling there.\n\nLet me check the reference structure at 21.0–21.6M: are there known segmental duplications in GRCh38 chr22? The chr22q11 region has famous LCRs (low-copy repeats) but those are at ~17–20M? The 20–22M region of chr22… I recall chr22 has a large block of segmental duplication. In any case let me analyze the reference directly: self-align 21.0–21.6M, find repeats (k-mers with high occ), identify duplicated blocks.\n\nActually — the cleanest test of the true deletion hypothesis is: look at the discordant pairs correctly. For the true deletion [20M,21M): spanning pairs should have R1 start < 20M (within ~insert of 20M) and R2 start ≥ 21M. For a deletion [21.16M,21.35M): spanning pairs have R1 < 21.16M, R2 ≥ 21.35M. Let me enumerate discordant pairs near these regions with uniqueness filters (e.g., both mates matches ≥ 140 to ensure unique placement) and see which junctions have support.\n\nAlso crucially: check whether pattern C split reads (20M|21M) are consistent with the depth edges: depth zero begins exactly at bin 2000 (20.0M) — bins 1998/1999 (19.98–20.0M) have ratios 0.636/0.626 — slightly low! Hmm, bins 1997–1999: 0.685, 0.636, 0.626 — ~63–68% of median. Suspicious: if the deletion starts at 20.0M, bins before it should be ~1.0. 0.63–0.69 is low. Hmm — but mfrac there is 0.98–1.0 so normalization is fine. Why are bins 1997–1999 low?\n\nOh wait. If the true left breakpoint is ~19.97M, and the simulator rounded to 20M… no. Hmm, or: reads from the left flank within [L-150, L) fail to map (they span the junction) — this only depletes the last 150bp of bin 1999 → negligible. Something else is lowering bins 1997–1999: ratio 0.685/0.636/0.626. Interesting — could be a heterozygous deletion overlapping? If the sample has a heterozygous deletion starting ~19.97M and a homozygous deletion… no…\n\nHmm, let me think: a ratio of ~0.63–0.69 is between het (0.5) and normal (1.0). Could be GC bias or mappability residual error, or real CNV. Bins 1995/1996: 1.540/0.837. Noisy.\n\nActually, hmm, wait: what if the true deletion is larger, e.g. [~19.97M? ...]. Let me check bins 1990–1999: 1.216, 0.866, 1.813, 0.915, 1.142, 1.540, 0.837, 0.685, 0.636, 0.626. Gradual decline toward the breakpoint… this might be an edge artifact: the expected value calculation is global; if the deleted region is large (1Mb+), the global rate = total_reads/total_mappable is slightly underestimated… no wait, that would uniformly raise all ratios.\n\nHmm, what could cause progressive decline before the breakpoint? If the sample has a deletion starting at 20.0M, then reads starting near 19.997M… no. Let me consider instead: could the true left breakpoint be at 19,970,000 or so, with partial… no, then bins would be 0.5 (het) or 0 (hom), not 0.63–0.69.\n\nOK, another thought — mapping losses: near the deletion breakpoint, reads containing junction sequence fail to map; that's only ~150bp. But reads from sequences with similarity to the deleted region (paralogs)… if the deleted 1Mb contains a repeat family also present near 19.97M, reads from the 19.97M copy might be ambiguously mapped — some get placed inside the deletion (and fail verification?)…\n\nLet me stop overinterpreting noise and instead look directly at the 19.97–20.0M region at 1kb resolution, and also enumerate the exact positions of the few reads mapping inside the deletion (the ~200 reads across bins 200–209) — these might be junction reads with partial matches, or reads from contaminant/paralog.\n\nAnother possibility: the deletion is not contiguous: the sample could have a complex rearrangement. But the task says \"a large deletion\" — likely the intended answer is [20,000,000, 21,000,000) size 1,000,000 (pattern C split reads at exact round coordinates + clean 1Mb depth trough is compelling).\n\nBut what about bin 212 (21.2–21.3M) zero, bins 2116–2134 zero, 2146–2155 zero, and the elevated bins? I need to explain those to be confident. Let me check the reference: is the 21.1–21.6M region of GRCh38 chr22 a known segmental duplication? Let me check the occ profile: fraction of k-mers with occ>16 in 10kb bins around 20–22M. In duplicated regions, mfrac should be low — actually bins 2110–2134 have mfrac 0.4–0.9 (not particularly low). Hmm.\n\nWait, actually, let me reconsider the \"normal patch\" 21.0–21.16M. If the true deletion is [20M, 21.16M)… no wait, then pattern C split reads with J_right=21,000,000 wouldn't exist… unless pattern C is the artifact. Ugh.\n\nLet me look at pattern C reads more carefully. R2#15240: J_left=20000000 (98/98 matches), J_right=21000000 (52/52 matches). A 150bp read with a 98bp prefix matching exactly at ref[20M-98:20M] and a 52bp suffix matching exactly at ref[21M:21M+52]. For this to be a true junction read, the sample must have the 20M|21M junction. For it to be an artifact, ref[20M-98:20M] would have to be similar to the true left flank of the true deletion AND ref[21M:21M+52] similar to the true right flank — i.e., the true deletion would have breakpoints at sequences paralogous to 20M and 21M. That's possible in a duplicated region!\n\nHmm, OK. Decisive test: find all split reads and discordant pairs without a region filter, cluster them by (J_left, J_right), and see which junctions are most supported. Currently my split-read detection was restricted to region [depth_left-300k, depth_right+300k] = [19.7M, 21.19M]. Let me rerun split detection genome-wide (no region filter) and cluster. If the true deletion is elsewhere, junction reads should cluster there.\n\nAlso the discordant pair analysis: filter by uniqueness (both mates matches ≥140), then cluster all discordant pairs genome-wide, count pairs per cluster. The true junction should have multiple pairs with consistent geometry. Noise pairs from repeats should scatter.\n\nWait, but earlier the genome-wide discordant analysis showed tons of clusters — mostly noise from repeat mismapping. With a uniqueness filter (matches ≥140 each), the noise should drop substantially.\n\nActually wait a moment, let me reconsider. Earlier genome-wide discordant clusters: many had \"left>=10537xxx\", \"left>=10550xxx\" etc. — position ~10.54M. Recall: the reference's first 10.51M is all Ns! So position 10.537M is just right of the p-arm gap. Reads mapping near 10.537M… that's the start of unique sequence on chr22q. Discordant pairs with left mate at ~10.537M and right mate scattered at 12–50M: these are reads anchored at the start of q-arm… hmm, or mismapped reads.\n\nAnd clusters with left ~10.6–10.7M, right ~16.2–16.9M: hmm, the 16.27–16.30M region has a 23kb N-gap and 1.1kb gap. The right flank of the centromeric gap is 15.15M…\n\nWhatever — noise from reads mapping near gaps/repeats. Uniqueness filter + cluster consistency should resolve.\n\nLet me write diag3:\n1. Load cache; rebuild index (needed for split search).\n2. Genome-wide split-read search (no region filter): both ends, primary (2 clusters) + secondary (partial reads). Cluster the results by (J_left, J_right) within 500bp. Report cluster counts.\n3. Discordant pairs with both mates matches ≥140: cluster genome-wide; report top clusters by pair count, with geometry (median s1, median s2, orientation).\n4. 1kb depth profile for 19.95–20.05M, 20.95–21.05M, 21.1–21.6M.\n5. Enumerate all reads mapping inside [20M,21M) and their match counts.\n\nThis should settle it. Runtime: split search genome-wide on all reads with ≥2 clusters — how many such reads? Unknown; could be thousands (repeat-induced 2-cluster reads). The per-read recomputation (_seed_diag across 14 offsets × pairwise) is cheap; the secondary search (partial reads: ~3900+4000 reads × 70 split points × _lookup_segments) might take 1–2 minutes. Acceptable.\n\nHmm, one issue: my find_split_reads has a `region=None` parameter that skips the region filter if None — good. split_from_partials requires a region parameter — I'll pass the whole chromosome (0, ref_len).\n\nAlso, for clustering split junctions: group by rounded (J_left//500, J_right//500).\n\nLet me also reconsider pattern B (21.129M|21.130M, span 1466): if real, that's a small 1.47kb deletion — but four reads with J_left scattered over 149bp suggests alignment slippage in a repeat: two copies 1466bp apart, reads from one copy map split across both copies. In fact, for a tandem duplication unit of length 1466: reads spanning the copy boundary would split-align with J_left = boundary - something… no. For a read within a tandem repeat array with copies A-B-C (each 1466bp), a read starting in copy A near its end could map its prefix to the end of copy A and suffix to the start of copy B — split alignment with J_left = end of A, J_right = start of B = end of A → span 0. Not 1466.\n\nHmm, span exactly 1466 = J_right - J_left: prefix maps at position X, suffix maps at X+1466 — that's consistent with a read from a region where the sequence at [X, X+s) is followed in the sample by sequence from [X+1466, ...). If the reference has two copies of a 1466bp unit at positions P and P+1466 (tandem), a read from the junction of a deletion removing exactly one unit [P+s, P+1466+s)… this is going in circles. Let me just look at the reference sequence structure at 21.128–21.131M: is ref[21128855:21130321] (1466bp) similar to the adjacent sequence? Compute: count of 21-mers in that 1466bp interval that have high occ or map nearby.\n\nLet me also print the actual sequences around these junctions and BLAST-like self-hits via the index.\n\ndiag3 plan (concrete):\n```python\n# 1. Genome-wide split search\nsplits_all = find_split_reads(codes1, rcc1, clusters1, ref_codes, index, \"R1\", None) + ... R2\nsplits_all += split_from_partials(codes1, (q1,m1,cr1,cs1,cp1), ref_codes, index, \"R1\", (0,ref_len)) + R2\n# cluster by (J_left//500, J_right//500)\n# 2. Discordant with uniqueness\nmask = (matches1>=140)&(matches2>=140) both mapped; FR; insert>1000\ncluster on (s1//500, s2//500); report top 20 by count\n# 3. 1kb depth around junctions\n# 4. Reads inside deletion\n# 5. Reference repeat structure at 21.128-21.131M: for the 1466bp segment, count k-mers by occurrence; also check if ref[21128855:21130321] equals ref elsewhere (exact-match copies via index lookup of all 21-mers).\n```\n\nWait, the cache has codes1/codes2 but not rcc/q/m/cr/cs/cp. find_split_reads needs rcc (compute quickly: rc of codes ~1 second). split_from_partials needs q_all/m_all/c_read/c_strand/c_start — not in cache! Need to rerun map_end to get those. map_end is ~5 seconds per end. OK — let me call map_end again (also get clusters).\n\nLet me write diag3."}}
