red-end hg38 data.\" The inputs reference README says the reference is chr22 obtained from the Broad hg38/v0 public reference via indexed byte-range fetch.\n\nHmm, hmm. Interesting: the Broad hg38 \"v0\"… hmm.\n\nWait, actually — I should check: could these split/discordant signals be false signals caused by segmental duplications in the real chr22? chr22 is the chromosome with the highest density of segmental duplications in the human genome! About 20% of chr22 consists of segmental duplications. Known large duplication blocks on chr22: the q11.2 region (17–20Mb) contains large LCRs; there are also blocks around 20–22Mb… The pattern: J_left 17.5–18.3M ↔ J_right 21.6–24.3M — could reflect paralogy between chr22q11.2 blocks and more distal blocks. If a read originates from copy A (~18M), its mate… no wait, these are split reads (single reads), not pairs.\n\nSplit read with J_left~18041000, J_right~21676500: a single 150bp read with prefix matching ref[18.04M..] and suffix matching ref[21.68M..]. For that to happen with high match counts (60/60+86/90), the read's sequence = ref[18041034:18041094] + ref[21676484:21676574]. Such a read would have to come from a sample that has a junction at 18.04M|21.68M… or the read is chimeric (PCR artifact?), or there's paralogy: the read actually comes from a single locus whose sequence is similar to the concatenation of two paralogous pieces — that is, there's a duplication block in the reference where copy 1 = [18.04M..X] is adjacent to unique sequence, and copy 2 = [21.68M..] hmm.\n\nHmm, wait, actually, here's the point: if the reference contains a segmental duplication block B present in two copies (positions P1 and P2), and the flanking unique sequences are different, then a read spanning the junction (unique flank F1 | block B) maps as a split read: one segment to F1, the other to B. If my split-point search tries both P1 and P2 for B, it would generate two split-read clusters, and one of them would have a huge apparent span 