{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_93","type":"reasoning","text":"Reads that map inside [20M, 21M) with high match counts (many 150/150!) — there are about 185 of them, clustered in specific hotspots: 20.27M, 20.31M, 20.37M, 20.53M, 20.67-20.69M, 20.76M, 20.91M, 20.97M, 20.05M, 20.005M, 20.03M, 20.04M…\n\nIf the deletion is truly a 1Mb homozygous deletion, these reads cannot possibly originate from the deleted region. They map there because the deleted sequence has nearly identical paralogs elsewhere in the genome (the reads originate from the paralogous loci preserved in the sample). This also explains the \"elevated\" bins elsewhere (the loci of origin, where both copies' reads pile up… no wait — the reads originate from the paralogous loci, and since the deleted copy doesn't produce reads, the paralogous locus should have normal depth (1.0) and the deleted region should be zero…\n\nHmm, wait. If the reads originate from paralogous locus P (preserved) and map equally well to P and to the deleted region D: my mapper places each read in one location or the other (arbitrary tiebreak). Then depth(P) ≈ 0.5x, depth(D) ≈ 0.5x. But we observe depth(D) ≈ 0.02x, and there are only about 185 reads within the 1Mb (not 0.5x = about 6,600 reads). So the tiebreak isn't 50/50… Interesting: the paralogous sequence inside D is similar to P but not identical (reads preferentially map to the truly identical locus P, and only reads with enough matching… hmm, but many of the reads inside D have 150/150 matches).\n\nWait — 150/150 matches inside the deleted region! That means ref[D_i : D_i+150] exactly matches those reads. If the reads originate from a paralogous locus P that is 100% identical to D over 150bp stretches, the reads map equally well to both — the diagonal voting tiebreak picks one. Hmm.\n\nOr — maybe I had it backwards: the sample's deletion isn't [20M,21M) of the reference. Maybe the sample has a sequence that isn't in the reference… no wait. Let me reconsider the whole thing.\n\nWhat if the sample genome is: reference chr22 with a deletion, but the deleted interval is not [20M,21M) — instead, the sample has lost a segment whose sequence appears elsewhere in the reference (i.e., the reference has a duplication and the sample deleted one copy)?\n\nHmm. Let me think: suppose the reference has two copies of a 1Mb segment: copy A is at [20M,21M), copy B is elsewhere (say at 21.16–22.16M, or 10.5M…, or wherever). The sample deleted copy A (the one at 20M-21M). Then:\n- Depth over [20M,21M) = 0 ✓ (homozygous deletion of copy A).\n- Reads originating from copy B map to B… but also equally to A (since identical): some map inside the deletion — matches perfectly (150/150) ✓ — consistent with the ~185 reads inside D! If copy B is about 1Mb and depth is 1x, then if mapping is a random tiebreak, we'd expect about half of B's reads (about 3,300) to map inside D… but we only see 185. Hmm, not 50/50. Unless the copies have diverged: most reads map uniquely to B (only one diagonal gets enough votes), only reads from the perfectly identical sub-stretches map ambiguously → a small fraction lands in D. ✓ Plausible!\n- The paralogous copy B should show roughly normal depth (since the deleted copy A doesn't contribute reads, B has depth 1.0 from its own reads; ambiguous reads split off a bit → about 0.95-1.0) ✓ consistent with bins 210/211 being normal…\n\nBut wait, where is copy B? The hotspots inside D (20.27, 20.31, 20.37, 20.67, 20.76…) — their paralogous loci of origin. And the depleted blocks [21.16,21.35), [21.46,21.55) + elevated blocks [21.36,21.46) — hmm.\n\nHmm, wait, actually, let me flip it: what if copy A (the one at 20-21M in the reference) is the preserved one, and the sample deleted copy B located at [21.16M, 21.35M) + [21.46M, 21.55M)?? No — those are small (190kb + 90kb), and the depth inside [20M,21M) is zero, meaning the sample has no copies of that sequence… unless the reads from copy A all map to copy B… no, then depth(A) would be zero only if A's reads all go to B, which requires B to exist and be identical… but B is deleted in the sample… circular.\n\nHmm, OK, wait. Actually, let me reconsider. Depth zero over [20M,21M) means: the sample has no sequence that maps uniquely to [20M,21M). Either (a) the sample deleted it (homozygous deletion), or (b) the sample has it but all reads originating from it map elsewhere (paralogy) — but in that case, depth at [20M,21M) would still get some tiebreak reads (as we see 185 of them) — and ratio 0.02 means about 2% of expected… actually, hmm: if the sample preserved [20M,21M) but ~98% of its reads map elsewhere due to near-identical paralogs, then… the paralogous loci would show about 2x depth. Do we see 2x depth somewhere? The elevated bins: 11.3M (1.96), 15.3M (2.01), 15.9M (2.07), 17.6M (1.90), 18.4M (2.32), 18.5M (2.40), 21.37-21.45M (1.5-5x)…\n\nInteresting!! The 18.4-18.5M region is 2.3-2.4x — near the 100kb N-gap at 18.24-18.34M. Hmm.\n\nHmm, let me think again from the simulation's perspective: the task creator took chr22, deleted a large segment from one haplotype (or both), and simulated reads. \"Distinguish evidence from precision limits\" and \"round breakpoints to the nearest 100kb\" suggests breakpoints at known exact positions, probably round numbers. The split reads at exactly 20,000,000/21,000,000 strongly suggest a 1Mb deletion at [20M,21M).\n\nSo then what about the depth at [21.16M,21.35M)? Let me reconsider: reads originating from 21.16-21.35M could be mismapped to paralogs… or the mappability normalization there is broken. Actually wait — I should check the raw counts: the fine bins at 2116-2134 had dep 0-35, but what about the expected count there? mfrac 0.4-0.9. dep is really low. Hmm.\n\nBut actually — wait. What if [21.16M, 21.35M) and [21.46M,21.55M) are paralogous to sub-stretches inside the deleted [20M,21M)? Then: in the sample, the 21.16-21.35M region is preserved; its reads map 50/50 (or with bias) between 21.16-21.35M and their copies inside the deletion. If reads preferentially map to the deleted copy (e.g., if the copy inside the deletion is the \"canonical\" one with higher k-mer uniqueness)… no, identical sequences tie.\n\nHmm, but the tiebreak in diagonal voting: np.unique sorts keys; the best cluster per read is picked by lexsort((-counts, u_rid)) — ties between two diagonals with the same count: does the first one in sorted order… does lexsort with keys (-counts, u_rid) preserve the original order of ukey for ties? np.lexsort is stable: for ties, the original order is preserved. The original order of ukey = np.unique(key) = sorted key order. key = ((rid<<1|strand)<<28)|diag. For the same read and strand, the smaller diag comes first → smaller position wins! So ambiguous reads preferentially map to the smaller position. If the paralogous pair is (D=20.3M, B=21.2M), the reads tie → they go to D (the smaller one). Then depth(D) gets about 50% of the reads from the B locus… but depth(D) is about 2%…\n\nExcept in the case where D and B aren't identical — say, they share only sub-stretches. Reads overlapping diverged bases vote correctly for B; only reads from perfectly identical stretches tie → go to D (the smaller one). The hotspots inside D (20.27, 20.31, 20.37, 20.67…) would be such identical stretches, and the paralogous B copies of those stretches would be at 21.16-21.35M etc.\n\nThen the depth at the B loci (21.16-21.35M) would be about 0.5x in the identical stretches (half the reads lost to D)… but the observed ratio is 0.02-0.15, much less than 0.5. Hmm. Unless the B loci themselves are also partly deleted in the sample??\n\nOK, this is too much speculation. Let me get the facts:\n\n1. Identify the paralogous origin of the 185 reads inside D: for each read, find all near-perfect alignment positions (all strong diagonals). If they have a second perfect position outside D, then they originate from there.\n\n2. Check whether the sequences of the depleted blocks [21.