{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_97","type":"reasoning","text":"Now, the situation is becoming both clearer and more complex:\n\n- Left junction: exactly 20,000,000 (depth drops from ~13-20/kb to zero).\n- Right junction: depth recovers exactly at 21,000,000 (30 reads at 21,000,000). In other words, the deletion is precisely [20,000,000, 21,000,000) — 1 Mb. Confirmed by 1kb resolution.\n\n- However: after 21.0M, depth is elevated: 21,114,000-21,134,000 has 40-73 reads/kb (against the usual ~15-25/kb!) — roughly 2-3x. Then around 21,135,000-21,150,000 it's roughly normal (17-38). After that, from 21,151,000 onward, it's zero (21.151M-21.175M+ is zero...).\n\nWait — this is interesting. Depth of 21.0M-21.15M: partially elevated (21.114-21.134 is ~2x). Next, zero from 21.151M onward (at least up to 21.175M; from earlier data, roughly up to 21.35M).\n\nHmm!! New hypothesis: the sample genome is not simply \"reference chr22 with a deletion.\" Let me think about what structure would produce this:\n1. Zero depth on [20M, 21M).\n2. Split reads spanning 20M|21M (perfect match).\n3. Depth of [21.0M, 21.15M) is roughly normal, but the sub-region [21.114M, 21.134M) is ~2-3x.\n4. Zero depth on [21.151M, ~21.35M) and [21.46M, 21.55M).\n5. Elevated [21.36M, 21.46M).\n\nHmm, wait — actually, hold on a moment. What if the sample genome is a reference chr22 with a deletion of [20M, 21M), AND the reads are... hmm, no.\n\nWhat if the simulator cut out [20M, 21M) from one haploid copy, but the data is... the data is shallow (~2x), consistent with a single haploid copy (or a homozygous deletion of both copies of the diploid). Mean Q 40, no errors — simulated.\n\nAlternatively: what if the \"deletion\" is not relative to this reference — that is, the simulator created the sample genome from the GRCh38 chr22 sequence, and the sample has a deletion, but the reads were simulated from a sample that also includes other changes?\n\nHmm, wait. Let me think about the ~2x elevated block