d... map to the true position with normal depth. The elevated block [21.114M, 21.134M) might be where reads from both the true locus and the inserted copy map (if the inserted copy is elsewhere...).\n\nHmm, hold on, let me reconsider: if the provided reference = true GRCh38 chr22, and the sample genome = GRCh38 chr22 with a deletion of [20M, 21M), but in addition, the reads were simulated with the task creator's own pipeline that introduced artifacts (chimeric reads, PCR duplicates)? The split reads of pattern B have an exact span of 1466 — that smells like the simulator's template. Four reads with a span of 1466...\n\nActually, let me check for duplicates in the read set: are there reads with identical sequences (PCR-duplicate-like)? The simulator may have amplified. And chimeric reads: the pattern A reads might be chimeric reads from the simulator (template switching) — but with consistent breakpoints? Hmm, no.\n\nOkay, another thought — perhaps the simplest: maybe the sample is not haploid: it's a diploid with a heterozygous deletion of [20M,21M) on one haplotype, and the other haplotype has a different structure... but the depth inside [20M,21M) is 0.005 — that's not heterozygous (which would be 0.5). Homozygous.\n\nHmm, let me look at the actual read sequences mapped inside the deletion, and their mates. If a read maps inside the deletion (perfectly), its pair's mate should map near the paralogous origin (if the reads come from the paralog). Check the mate's position of reads mapped inside [20M,21M): if mates cluster in specific regions (e.g., ~21.15-21.35M, ~18.4M...), that reveals the paralogous origin.\n\nWait, hold on. Actually — the discordant pair clusters I saw earlier: \"n=3 left~20670000 right~22117500\": left mate is at 20.67M (inside the deletion!), right mate is at 22.1M. And \"n=4 left~21134500 right~23330500\" hmm.\n\nLet me check the mates of the reads inside the deletion. If the read at 20.67M is on the + strand and its mate maps at ~22.1M on the − strand with an insert of ~1.4Mb — that \"pair\" geometry is consistent with a molecule spanning... no, a molecule from the paralogous locus (say, a duplication of [20.6-20.7M] at [22.0-22.1M] and [23.5-23.6M])... if the paralogous locus is a duplication of a segment from inside the deletion, the pairs from there should have normal insert (500bp) and map within the paralogous locus; but one mate might map ambiguously inside the deletion (the smaller position wins), while the other mate (containing unique flanking sequence) maps at the true paralog position → producing apparent long-range discordant pairs (left inside the deletion, right at the paralog position)!!\n\nYes — that would explain: discordant clusters with left inside [20M,21M) (e.g., left~20670000) and right at 22.1M, and split reads with J_left inside the deletion / J_right at 21.17-21.18M: these are all artifacts of reads originating from the paralogous locus outside the deletion, one segment of which maps inside the deleted region (perfect paralog).\n\nSo: the reference has a segmental duplication: sequences inside [20M,21M) (the deleted region) also exist at:\n- ~[21.15M, 21.35M)? (pattern A's J_right is ~21.17-21.18M; discordant right ends are ~22.1M, ~23.3M, ~24.2M...)\n\nHmm, multiple paralogous loci. This is getting complex, but the key conclusion doesn't change: the primary event is the deletion of [20M, 21M), confirmed by:\n1. Clean depth trough with sharp edges exactly at 20,000,000 and 21,000,000 (1kb resolution).\n2. Split reads spanning exactly 20,000,000|21,000,000, with near-perfect matches.\n3. Round-number coordinates (simulator signature).\n\nRemaining issues:\n(a) Zero depth on [21.151M, 21.35M), [21.46M, 21.55M), elevated depth on [21.114M,21.134M), [21.36M,21.46M) — needs to be explained to rule out additional events.\n(b) Pattern B split reads (21.129M|21.130M, span 1466) — needs to be explained.\n\nFor (a): if the reference contains additional copies of sequences paralogous to t