.46M,21.55M)... no wait, then coordinates wouldn't be GRCh38...\n\nHmm, or the other way around: the reference has a deletion relative to the true genome... no.\n\nHmm, hold on. Let me reconsider. What if the sample genome has an inversion or translocation, and the \"deletion\" I'm seeing is a mapping artifact?? No — the split reads spanning 20M|21M with perfect matches are direct evidence of a junction in the sample.\n\nAlright, let me test directly: does ref[21151000:21350000] match anywhere else in the reference (paralogy)? And does ref[20000000:21000000] (the deleted segment) have paralogous copies elsewhere? Use the index: for each k-mer in the query, count occ>1. Let me write diag4:\n\n1. Paralogy profile: slide across chr22 in 10kb windows: fraction of k-mers with occ>1 and occ>16. Print the profile for 19.5M-22M, and find genome-wide windows with a high fraction of occ 2-16 (candidate duplication blocks).\n2. Specifically: for the deleted region [20M,21M): fraction of k-mers with occ≥2, and where those extra copies are (histogram of the second position).\n3. For [21.151M,21.35M): the same.\n4. Mates of reads inside the deletion: where do they map?\n\nThis should resolve the structure. Let me also reconsider pattern B: J_left~21.129M, J_right~21.130M, span exactly 1466, four reads — this is inside the elevated [21.114M,21.134M) block. If that elevated block is a tandem duplication in the reference (two adjacent copies of a ~20kb unit?), reads spanning the copy boundary would split-map with span ≈ unit length... for a tandem duplication with unit length U: reference ...[U1][U2]...; a read crossing the boundary U1|U2: prefix matches the end of U1, suffix matches the start of U2. But U1≈U2, so the suffix also matches the start of U1... split alignment: J_left = boundary - (position within the unit), hmm. Let me think: read = ref[P-α : P] + ref[P : P+β], where P is the boundary. The prefix ref[P-α:P] matches the end of U1 (unique within the unit?) and also the en