{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_100","type":"reasoning","text":"OK, this is now quite clear:\n\n**Section A**: Offset 828941 self-similarity = 37.6% identity — random. So Pattern A is NOT a tandem duplication at that offset. Hmm.\n\n**Section B**: Offset 1466 = 33.8% identity — random! So Pattern B is also NOT a tandem duplication. Interesting — so why do reads split-map with an exact span of 1466?\n\n**Section C**: The k-mer occurrence profile reveals it:\n- [21.15M, 21.35M): frac_ge2 = 0.91–0.93 — over 90% of k-mers occur 2 or more times! This is a large segmental duplication block.\n- [21.45M, 21.55M): frac_ge2 = 0.83–0.95 — another duplication block.\n- The deleted region [20M,21M) has variable repetitiveness (frac_ge2 0.02–0.5).\n\n**Section D**: The k-mers of the deleted region have extra copies mainly in the following: [21.1M,21.2M) (620 hits!), [18.4M,18.6M) (563+466), [18.7M,18.8M) (437), [21.4M,21.5M) (433), [24.2M,24.3M), [21.2M,21.3M) (187), and several others. In other words, the sequence of the deleted region [20M,21M) has paralogous copies at approximately 21.1–21.2M, 18.4–18.8M, 21.4–21.5M, 24.2M, etc. This is chr22's segmental duplication architecture.\n\n**Section E**: Of the k-mers in [21.151M,21.35M), 23% also appear inside [20M,21M) — paralogy confirmed.\n\n**Section F**: The mates of the reads inside the deletion map to the following bins: 181 (=18.1M? No wait — bin 181 = 18.1M), 189 (=18.9M), 221 (=22.1M), 206 (=20.6M?? inside the deletion!), 242 (=24.2M), 210 (=21.0M), 246 (=24.6M)...\n\nWait: bin 206 = 20.6M — inside the deletion! Some reads have both mates inside the deletion. And 181 = 18.1–18.2M (hmm, near the 18.24–18.34M N-gap), 189 = 18.9M, 221 = 22.1M, 242 = 24.2M.\n\nHmm wait, actually: mates mapping to bin 206 (20.6M, inside the deletion): pairs with both reads inside the deletion — these are fragments that are entirely within the paralogous sequence; both mates tie, both get sent to the smaller position inside the deletion.\n\nOK, so the overall picture is this: chr22 has a complex segmental duplication architecture around 18–24M (consistent with known chr22q11-q12 duplications). The sample has a homozygous deletion of [20,000,000, 21,000,000). Reads originating from the paralogous copies (preserved in the sample: 18.4–18.9M, 21.1–21.35M, 21.4–21.5M, 24.2M...) sometimes map inside the deleted region due to k-mer voting ties (the smaller position wins), producing ~185 stray reads and Pattern A split reads (one segment inside the deletion, one at a paralogous position).\n\nThe depth drop in [21.151M,21.35M): the reads originating there mostly map to the paralogous positions inside the deletion? No wait — that would inflate depth inside the deletion. Hmm, the actual tiebreak: smaller position wins. Positions inside the deletion (20.xM) are smaller than 21.15M. So for a read originating at 21.15–21.35M that ties with a position inside the deletion: gets sent to 20.xM. But we only see 185 reads inside the deletion... For reads originating at 21.15–21.35M (expected ~3000 reads), most seeds should vote uniquely for 21.15–21.35M (because the paralogs have diverged), and only reads where enough seeds tie get mis-sent. Hmm, but if so, depth at 21.15–21.35M should be nearly normal minus a small loss. The observation is ~2%.\n\nHmm, wait — let me reconsider. In the 21.15–21.35M region, frac_ge2 = 0.91–0.93, but the mean occ is 25–36 — meaning k-mers there have many copies (a multi-copy repeat family, occ often >16). For occ>16, seeds get discarded. Reads from that region have few usable seeds → weak clusters → the best cluster might be at a paralogous position where more seeds happen to vote... or the reads fail to map... or get placed at the smallest tied position.\n\nActually wait — maybe a cleaner explanation for the drop in [21.151M, 21.35M): the reads originating there tie among multiple paralogous positions (2–4 copies with occ in the range ≤16), and the deterministic tiebreak sends almost all of them to the smallest position in the set. If the smallest paralogous position for most of these reads is inside the deleted [20M,21M)... hmm, but then depth inside the deletion should spike up to ~0.5–1x, and it doesn't.\n\nHmm wait a moment, let me re-verify: is depth inside the deletion really only ~185 reads? Coarse bins 200–209: dep = 8+1+24+44+7+9+73+10+3+6 = 185. That's right.\n\nSo where do the reads originating from [21.151M,21.35M) go? Options: (a) unmapped (no valid seeds / cluster fails verification), (b) mapped to other paralogous positions (18.4–18.8M? 21.4–21.5M? 24.2M?).\n\nRecall the elevated coarse bins: 18.4M (ratio 2.32), 18.5M (2.40), 11.3M (1.96), 15.3M (2.01), 15.9M (2.07), 17.6M (1.90). And the fine-scale elevated blocks: [21.114M,21.134M) (~2–3x), [21.36M,21.46M) (~1.5–5x), [21.56M,21.9M) (~1–2.7x).\n\nSo the reads originating from the 21.15–21.35M duplication block probably map to the paralogous blocks at 18.4–18.6M (which are elevated!) and/or 21.114–21.134M and 21.36–21.46M. The direction of the tiebreak depends on which positions tie — among {18.4M, 20.xM, 21.15M, 21.4M, 24.2M...}, the smallest wins → 18.4M wins over 20.xM! So reads tying among these copies go to 18.4M (explaining ratio 2.3–2.4 there), not inside the deletion (consistent with few reads inside the deletion)!\n\nHmm OK wait, but then why is the [21.36M,21.46M) block elevated (1.5–5x)? Reads from [21.46M,21.55M) (the drop, frac_ge2 0.83–0.95) tie with the copy at 21.36–21.46M (the smaller one) → go there. And [21.151M,21.35M) reads tie among {18.4M..., 21.114M?, 21.36M?} → some go to the 21.114–21.134M block (2–3x elevation ✓).\n\nWhatever the exact wiring, it's all explained by segmental duplications + deterministic tiebreak mapping. The depth artifacts (drops and elevations outside [20M,21M)) are mapping artifacts in duplicated regions, not real CNVs — because the reads are actually present (they just get placed at the wrong paralogous position). The strongest evidence that the drops at 21.15M+ are artifacts: the total read count is conserved (elevations elsewhere compensate), and the pattern correlates with the frac_ge2≥0.9 duplication blocks.\n\nHmm wait, but let me double-check the claim that reads from 21.15–21.35M map elsewhere rather than being absent from the sample. If the sample had two deletions ([20M,21M) and [21.15M,21.35M)), reads originating from 21.15–21.35M wouldn't exist; in that case, the elevations at 18.4M/21.114M/21.36M would have to be explained by yet other paralogs... and Pattern A split reads have their right segment mapping at 21.17–21.18M — a read whose suffix maps at 21.17–21.18M with high identity (90–120 matches out of 90–120). If [21.15M,21.35M) were deleted from the sample, reads couldn't have a suffix matching there... unless the suffix comes from yet another paralog (e.g., a copy at 24.2M or 18.4M maps equivalently to 21.17M). Given the multi-copy family (mean occ 25–36), that's entirely plausible.\n\nHmm. OK, so which is it? Two deletions, or one deletion + mapping artifacts? Let me think about the total read count / global dosage. If the sample deleted [20M,21M) (1Mb), the total mappable read count decreases by ~1Mb × 2x / 300bp... The global ratio median is 0.983 — no problem.\n\nTest: look at insert sizes / pairs in the 21.15–21.35M region: if the sample has that region, concordant pairs should map there (both mates within the region, FR, insert ~500). Even if single-read placement is ambiguous, pairs where both mates map consistently within the region are strong evidence of presence. Let me count concordant FR pairs with both starts in [21.151M,21.35M). If the sample has the region, there should be ~(200kb × 2x)/500 ≈ 800 fragments ≈ hundreds of concordant pairs there. If deleted, ~0 (except for mismapped paralogs — but concordant pairs with both mates inside are unlikely to be mismapped, unless both paralogous copies tie to the same position... possible but rare).\n\nSimilarly, count