[20M-150, 20M) = 0.15kb out of 10kb = 1.5% loss. No.\n\nOr: reads from [19.97M,20M) partially tie with paralogs inside the deleted region (some fraction of reads get sent inside the deletion → the flanking bin loses depth!). Section D showed that paralogous copies of deleted-region k-mers concentrate at 21.1M/18.4M/etc. — but what about paralogous copies of left-flank sequence? Hmm, if ref[19.97M:20M) has paralogs elsewhere (e.g., inside the deleted region or elsewhere), reads originating from 19.97–20M might partially map elsewhere → local depth decreases. Plausible!\n\nLet me check at 1kb resolution: is the dip at 19.97–20.0M real and sharp? From diag3: bins 19960–19999 (19.96–20.0M): 27,16,18,29,12,16,19,12,24,12,17,14,29,28,15,9,16,13,10,16,24,12,20,13,12,17,15,15,14,13,20,15,8,15,19,7,23,11,18,13 — mean ~16.5, fairly uniform, no visible trend toward 20.0M. So at 1kb, no drop; the 100kb bin ratio of ~0.65 must reflect a local expected-value issue (local mappability vs global rate: those bins might truly have lower alignability). Well — not a deletion signal.\n\nOK, the decisive test (diag5):\n1. Concordant FR pairs with both starts in [21.151M,21.35M) and [21.46M,21.55M) vs. control regions.\n2. Also verify that concordant pairs are ~0 inside [20M,21M).\n3. Also count the number of reads mapped in those regions with match count ≥145.\n\nIf [21.151M,21.35M) is present in the sample → concordant pairs there (hundreds). If absent → ~0.\n\nNext, finalize the deletion call: [20,000,000, 21,000,000), size 1,000,000.\n\n1-based coordinates: POS (last retained base) = 20,000,000 (0-based index 19,999,999 → 1-based 20,000,000 ✓); END (last deleted base) = 0-based 20,999,999 → 1-based 21,000,000. size = 1,000,000. Round to the nearest 100kb: already exact. start_100kb = 20000000, end_100kb = 21000000, size_bp = 1000000.\n\nWait — my convention in the code: pos1 = left_raw (0-based start of the deleted interval = 20,000,000) — as the \"last retained base, 1-based\" this equals 20,000,000 ✓ (since 0-based 19,999,999 + 1 = 20,000,000). end1 = right_raw (0-based exclusive end = 21,000,000) = 1-based last deleted base 21,000,000 ✓. size = end1 - pos1 = 1,000,000 ✓.\n\nAlso, let me look more closely at the split reads spanning the junction — the exact breakpoint from the split reads: R2#15240: J_left=20000000 (prefix 98bp), J_right=21000000 (suffix 52bp); R2#152440: J_left=20000001, J_right=21000001 (100+50); R1#139652: J_left=20000000, J_right=21000000 (44+106). The +1 jitter in one read is typical alignment slippage (microhomology at the breakpoint). Median → 20,000,000 / 21,000,000.\n\nBut my current analysis.py's integration takes the median of all split reads including Pattern A/B junk → giving 20,400,000/21,100,000. analysis.py needs to be fixed as follows:\n1. Filter split reads: require J_left/J_right consistency with the depth interval (J_left within [left_edge-5kb, left_edge+5kb]... better: require J_left to be in the left-flank region and J_right in the right-flank region of the depth-called interval; and/or cluster split reads and pick the cluster that brackets the depth interval).\n2. Filter discordant pairs by uniqueness (matches ≥ 140 for both mates) and require them to bracket the depth interval.\n3. Also, the \"raw\" breakpoint from split reads = 20,000,000 / 21,000,000 exactly.\n\nLet me first run the diag5 pair test, then rewrite the integration portion of analysis.py."}}
