{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_103","type":"reasoning","text":"Interesting! Concordant pair test:\n- Deletion [20M,21M): 7 concordant pairs (background level of mismapping) ✓ Deleted.\n- Block [21.151M,21.35M): 7 concordant pairs — essentially zero, same as the deleted region!!\n- Block [21.46M,21.55M): 0 concordant pairs!\n- Controls: about 900 pairs per 100kb.\n- Elevated [21.36M,21.46M): 469/100kb (about half of control — reads from two loci converging).\n- Elevated [18.4M,18.6M): 682/100kb.\n\nHmm!! So [21.151M,21.35M) and [21.46M,21.55M) also have ~0 concordant pairs — same signature as the genuine deletion! Does this mean these regions are also deleted in the sample?? Or is it that because of the multi-copy paralogy (mean occ 25-36), reads from these regions simply can't form concordant pairs that map there (both mates get placed elsewhere)?\n\nHmm. If these regions are present in the sample and reads originate from them, then even if the placement is ambiguous, some fraction of pairs should have both mates placed in the region (either by tiebreak or by unique anchoring). 7 pairs in 200kb vs 900/100kb in controls — about 0.4% of expected. That's not \"some fraction\" — it's effectively zero.\n\nHmm wait, but hold on — could this be an artifact of my tiebreak sending everything to 18.4M (the smallest paralog)? For a pair to be concordant within [21.151M,21.35M), both mates would have to be placed there. If the tiebreak systematically prefers 18.4M over 21.15M, then pairs originating from 21.15M would have both mates sent to 18.4M → concordant pairs at 18.4M (which we see: 682/100kb vs expected ~450 for a single-copy 200kb… hmm, 682 vs control 900: not clearly elevated).\n\nHmm, confusing. Let me think about how to distinguish:\n(a) Sample deleted [21.151M,21.35M) (in addition to [20M,21M)).\n(b) Sample retains it, but multi-copy paralogy scrambles the placement.\n\nTest: look at reads placed at 18.4-18.6M (elevated, 682 pairs/100kb): are those pairs consistent with originating from 18.4-18.6M, or do they look like refugees? Also, total dosage: total read count of the sample = 332,123 pairs. Haploid genome size with deletion ≈ 50.8M - 13.7M(N) - 1M(deletion) ≈ 36.1M. Expected pairs = 36.1M × … let me compute expected: 2x of unique… let me compute expected: 332123×2×150 = 99.6M bp / ~36M effective = 2.76x. Hmm, the earlier \"mean depth\" qc was ~2x.\n\nGlobal test for (a) vs (b): if [21.151M,21.35M)+[21.46M,21.55M) (290kb) is also deleted, then the total sequenced unique content decreases correspondingly; depth elsewhere doesn't change. Can't easily distinguish.\n\nBetter test: split-read evidence. If [21.151M,21.35M) is a genuine deletion, there should be junction reads spanning its breakpoints (at ~21.151M and ~21.35M). Junction reads would split-map with J_left ≈ 21.151M and J_right ≈ 21.35M. Do I see such split reads? From the diag3 primary list: clusters at 21.15-21.35M… I see \"n=4 left~21134500 right~23330500\" (discordant pairs), split clusters… hmm, the top-30 primary split list doesn't include a (21.151M, 21.35M) cluster. Let me search specifically for split reads/discordant pairs bracketing [21.151M,21.35M) and [21.46M,21.55M).\n\nAlso, discordant pairs spanning the primary deletion [20M,21M): left <20M, right ≥21M — check with uniqueness filter! If the [20M,21M) deletion is real, spanning pairs should exist: expected number ≈ fragment coverage × … ~0.86×(2 junctions)… ~1-3 pairs. Let me look for those specifically — that would be beautiful corroboration. From the diag3 discordant list: \"n=3 left~20670000 right~22117500\" — no. Hmm, I don't see any left~19.9x, right~21.0x clusters. Let me search specifically.\n\nAlso, the pattern B split reads (21.129M|21.130M, span 1466, 4 reads) — if [21.151M,21.35M) is deleted… 21.129-21.130M is outside that block (21.129 < 21.151). The 21.114-21.134M block is \"elevated\" in depth. Pattern B is probably still an artifact.\n\nOK, let me think about the simulation design. Task: \"Identify large deletion in provided low-coverage paired-end hg38 data.\" Singular, \"large deletion.\" Simulator: probably took GRCh38 chr22, cut out one segment, simulated reads. Breakpoints are round numbers: 20,000,000 / 21,000,000 — clean. Everything else (the 21.1-21.6M chaos) is probably an intrinsic feature of chr22's segmental duplications confusing my simple mapper — and probably also confuses naive depth-based callers (that's the point of the benchmark: \"distinguish evidence from precision limits\"? Hmm, not exactly, but OK).\n\nBut wait — the concordant-pair count of ~0 in [21.151M,21.35M) really bothers me. Let me reconsider whether reads originating there could even map there in the first place. frac_ge2 = 0.91-0.93: 91-93% of k-mers have ≥2 copies. Mean occ 25-36 (many >16 → seeds discarded). Usable seeds per read from that region: only k-mers with occ ≤16, maybe 7-9% of seeds… ~1 seed per read. With ~1 informative seed, voting produces a single cluster of 1-2 votes; candidate placement = that diagonal; validation requires ≥130/150 matches — passes at true position and paralogous positions; the tiebreak picks the smallest tied position. If the smallest paralogous position is 18.4M or inside [20M,21M), the read goes there. So yes — reads originating from [21.151M,21.35M) systematically map elsewhere. Concordant pairs within the region ≈ 0 even if the region is present. My test (a)/(b) can't be resolved with concordant pairs alone for multi-copy regions.\n\nResolving instead with total pair count conservation: if the region is present, its reads must map somewhere — the elevated regions (18.4-18.6M: 682 pairs/100kb vs expected ~450/100kb… wait, expected pairs per 100kb at 2.76x coverage ≈ 2.76×100000/500 ≈ 552 pairs/100kb? Observed controls ~880-900/100kb. Hmm, let me recompute: controls show ~900 pairs/100kb. So expected ~900/100kb. 18.4-18.6M: 682/100kb — actually lower than control, not elevated?! But read count there was elevated (ratio 2.3-2.4 in depth)! Because depth counts read starts (single reads), while concordant pairs require both mates to be consistently placed. In the duplication block, single-read placement inflates but pair consistency breaks down.\n\nOK, let me do the dosage balance computation properly. If sample = chr22 minus [20M,21M) only:\n- Total mappable unique content: roughly constant.\n- Regions 21.151-21.35M, 21.46-21.55M present → their reads (~290kb × 2.76x ≈ 800 fragments × 2 reads ≈ 1600 reads) get redistributed to paralogous positions (18.4-18.9M, 21.1-21.13M, 21.36-21.46M, etc.).\n\nIf sample = chr22 minus [20M,21M) minus [21.151M,21.35M) minus [21.46M,21.55M): those 1600 reads don't exist.\n\nTotal mapped reads: 655,350 (98.7%+98.6%). Hmm, hard to calibrate absolutely.\n\nAlternative approach — look for the actual junction reads of the candidate secondary deletions:\n- For [21.151M,21.35M): junction reads would be ref[...21151000]+ref[21350000...]. Search split reads with J_left ∈ [21.14M,21.16M] and J_right ∈ [21.34M,21.36M]. Also discordant pairs with left mate ∈ [21.1M,21.151M] and right mate ∈ [21.35M,21.4M].\n- For [21.46M,21.55M): J_left ∈ [21.45M,21.47M], J_right ∈ [21.54M,21.56M].\n\nFrom diag3's primary split list (top 30 by count), I didn't see such clusters, but the list was truncated at 30. Let me scan all 5854 primary splits and all secondary splits for junctions matching these candidates, and also scan discordant pairs spanning them.\n\nAlso — search for discordant pairs spanning the primary deletion: s1 ∈ [19.9M,20.0M), s2 ∈ [21.0M,21.1M). Even 1-2 pairs would add evidence.\n\nLet me write diag6."}}
