{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_107","type":"reasoning","text":"Now we have solid evidence:\n\n1. **Discordant pairs spanning the primary deletion**: 1 pair — s1=19,999,645 (forward strand), s2=21,000,058 (reverse strand), insert size = 1,000,563. This pair brackets exactly the [20M, 21M) junction: R1 ends at 19,999,795 (155bp short of 20M... wait, R1 start 19,999,645 + 150 = 19,999,795 ≤ 20,000,000 ✓), R2 starts at 21,000,058 ≥ 21,000,000 ✓. A perfect spanning pair for the 1Mb deletion!\n\n2. **Split reads at the primary junction** (from diag3): 3 reads with J_left=20,000,000/20,000,001, J_right=21,000,000/21,000,001, near-perfect matches.\n\n3. **No split reads and no discordant pairs** spanning the candidate secondary blocks [21.151M,21.35M) and [21.46M,21.55M) — despite those blocks having depth near zero. If those were genuine deletions in the sample, at ~2.7x coverage we'd expect ~2-3 junction reads and ~1 spanning pair for each. Zero observed. Combined with the fact that these blocks are 91-95% repetitive (frac_ge2 0.83-0.95, average occ 25-36), the most consistent explanation is: reads originating from these duplicated blocks are being placed at paralogous positions by the mapper (deterministic tie-break among nearly identical copies) — a mapping artifact, not a deletion.\n\nHmm, wait, but I should double-check this claim: if the block is present, wouldn't at least some of the reads map there? frac_ge2=0.91 means 91% of the k-mers have 2 or more copies — but the reads need seeds with occ≤16; k-mers with average occ 25-36: a substantial fraction exceeds 16 (frac_gt16 = 0.05-0.19). The remaining ~80-95% have occ 2-16 (usable seeds, but ambiguous among 2-16 positions). Voting: seeds scatter across 2-16 diagonals; the best diagonal typically receives ~30-50% of the seeds; my mapper picks the best cluster regardless of the number of votes, then verifies (≥130/150 matches — passes since the copies are nearly identical). Tie-break among equal vote counts → smallest position. If a block's reads vote among copies {18.4M, 20.3M, 21.15M, 21.4M, 24.2M}, the winner depends on the vote count: if votes are exactly equal, smallest wins (20.3M — inside the deletion!). But if votes are unequal (due to copy-specific variants), the majority wins — could be any copy. The observation (only 119 unique-match reads in the 20-21M block, 84 in the 21.15-21.35M block) suggests that the majority of reads from these families land elsewhere (the elevation at 18.4M: 5575 unique reads vs ~3600 for control — excess ≈ 2000 reads ≈ roughly consistent with the redistribution of ~3000 reads from the 21.15-21.35M block, minus those sent elsewhere).\n\nGood. So: single large deletion [20,000,000, 21,000,000), homozygous, size 1,000,000 bp. The other depth dips are artifacts of the segmental duplication mapping, and I'll document this in the report as a caveat distinguishing evidence from precision limits.\n\nOne more check on the primary deletion: left breakpoint exactly 20,000,000 — split reads show J_left=20,000,000 (two reads) and 20,000,001 (one read); depth at 1kb resolution: 19,999,000-20,000,000 bin has 13 reads, 20,000,000-20,001,000 has 0. Right breakpoint: J_right=21,000,000 (two reads), 21,000,001 (one read); depth at 20,999,000-21,000,000: 0 reads, 21,000,000-21,001,000: 30 reads. Discordant pair: s1=19,999,645 (ends at 19,999,795), s2=21,000,058. All consistent with breakpoints at exactly 20,000,000 / 21,000,000 (the slight +1 wobble from microhomology/alignment).\n\nRaw estimate (0-based half-open): [20,000,000, 21,000,000). 1-based event: last retained base = 20,000,000 (1-based), last deleted base = 21,000,000 (1-based). Size = 1,000,000 bp. Rounded to 100kb: same (already exact multiples of 100kb).\n\nHmm, one consideration about coordinate interpretation: if the simulator deleted 0-based [20,000,000, 21,000,000) (i.e., 1-based positions 20,000,001..21,000,000), then \"start\" 1-based inclusive = 20,000,001, end = 21,000,000. Rounded to the nearest 100kb: start → 20,000,000, end → 21,000,000. Same answer. Good — robust to ±1 conventions.\n\nNow, update analysis.py to generate the correct final answer:\n\nKey fixes to analysis.py:\n1. **Split read filtering**: The current code takes the median of all split reads (including junk patterns). Fix: select split reads that bracket the depth-called interval: J_left within [depth_left_edge - 50kb, depth_left_edge + 50kb]... hmm — actually better: require J_left ≤ depth interval left edge region and J_right ≥ right edge region... let me define: for the depth interval [L, R) (coarse bins × 100kb), accept a split read if J_left ∈ [L-10000, L+10000]... no wait — J_left is the left breakpoint, should be ≈ L (20.0M), and J_right ≈ R (21.0M). But due to edge resolution, L from depth is 20.0M and R = 21.0M (coarse bins [200,210) → [20.0M, 21.0M)). Accept split reads with J_left ∈ [L-50kb, L+50kb] and J_right ∈ [R-50kb, R+50kb]. Pattern A (J_left 20.34M) is excluded ✓, Pattern B (21.129M) is excluded ✓. Pattern C is included ✓.\n\nHmm, but caveat: the fine edge of the depth interval from call_deletion_interval was [20.0M, 20.89M) (fine run stopped early due to bins with mfrac 0.23 < 0.3 at 2089-2099 → fv False). For the split filter, use the coarse interval [b0*100kb, b1*100kb) = [20.0M, 21.0M). OK.\n\n2. **Filter discordant pairs by uniqueness** (both mates matches ≥ 140) AND require spanning the depth interval: s1 ∈ [L-20kb, L+10kb)... hmm, actually spanning means s1 < L (left flank) and s2 ≥ R (right flank): s1+150 ≤ L+1000 and s2 ≥ R-1000, plus insert > threshold. Keep clustering for QC, but the primary evidence = spanning pairs across the called interval.\n\n3. **Integration priority**: split reads spanning the interval → raw breakpoints = median of J_left/J_right. Otherwise use discordant bracket. Otherwise depth edges.\n\n4. **Zygosity**: inner ratio 0.006 → homozygous ✓.\n\n5. **report.md + qc.json**: include: primary deletion call, raw evidence (split reads listed, spanning pair, depth edges at 1kb), plus a caveat section on segmental duplications explaining secondary depth dips (21.15-21.35M, 21.46-21.55M) and spurious split clusters (Pattern A/B) as mapping ambiguity — explicitly \"evidence vs precision limits\": the reported coordinates are rounded to 100kb (±50kb), whereas raw evidence places breakpoints at 20,000,000/21,000,000 (within ~200bp from discordant bracket, at nucleotide level from split reads); depth bins alone would give ±10kb.\n\nAlso, fix the fine-edge computation issue? The fine edge [20.0M, 20.89M) is slightly wrong (should be 21.0M) because bins 2089-2099 had mfrac 0.23 → excluded. Actually mfrac 0.23 means that 10kb bin has only 23% mappable starts — but dep there = 0, expected = mb×rate ≈ 2300×0.187... hmm, expected >5 so ratio=0 → should be \"low\" and included. The problem is the `fv = mfrac > 0.3` filter excludes those bins → flow=False → run stops. Fix: in the fine refinement, don't require mfrac>0.3 for bins whose dep is literally 0... or simply: treat bins with dep==0 and mb>0 as low regardless of mfrac. Actually more simply: define flow = (fr < 0.62*f_med) & (mb > 500 or dep == 0)... let me implement: flow = fv & low OR (dep == 0 & mb >= 200). Hmm — bins at gap edges (N regions) have mb=0 → excluded ✓. Bins 2089-2099: mb ≈ 2300, dep 0 → included ✓. Let me add that.\n\nThen fine edges = [20.0M, 21.0M) exactly (bins 2090-2099 are low; is bin 2100 (21.0-21.01M) also low? bin 2100 dep=30 → ratio ~0.16... hmm, might be <0.62*f_med → included → right edge becomes 21.01M or later!). Watch out: the fine run can bleed into flanks where depth partially recovers: bin 2100: dep 30 (expected ~122) → ratio 0.25 < 0.62 → \"low\" → run extends to include 2100; bin 2101: dep 13 → ratio ~0.1 low!; bin 2102: 15 → low... wait — from 1kb profile, bins 21000-21040 have dep 6-34/1kb ≈ ~15-20/10kb... in the earlier fine output bin 2101 (21.01-21.02M): dep=13? Hmm, no wait, from the diag2 fine list I saw